1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,698
Your screen isn't playing a video.

2
00:00:01,718 --> 00:00:03,245
It's flipping still pictures.

3
00:00:03,437 --> 00:00:06,844
60, 120, even 144 every second.

4
00:00:07,198 --> 00:00:08,246
Two numbers decide how

5
00:00:08,266 --> 00:00:09,352
smooth it feels.

6
00:00:09,706 --> 00:00:10,966
Frames per second:

7
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:13,137
how fast your chip draws pictures.

8
00:00:13,491 --> 00:00:14,263
Hertz:

9
00:00:14,617 --> 00:00:16,620
how often your screen refreshes.

10
00:00:16,974 --> 00:00:18,417
At 60, each picture gets

11
00:00:18,437 --> 00:00:20,243
16.7 milliseconds.

12
00:00:20,329 --> 00:00:22,260
At 120, just 8.3.

13
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:24,167
At 144, under seven.

14
00:00:24,521 --> 00:00:25,348
Every refresh,

15
00:00:25,368 --> 00:00:27,522
the screen redraws itself row by row.

16
00:00:27,795 --> 00:00:28,703
Inside each pixel,

17
00:00:28,723 --> 00:00:30,247
tiny transistors hold its color

18
00:00:30,267 --> 00:00:31,597
until the next refresh.

19
00:00:32,055 --> 00:00:33,533
This video only plays at 60,

20
00:00:33,553 --> 00:00:35,103
so let's slow time down.

21
00:00:35,457 --> 00:00:37,179
At 60 hertz, a moving ball

22
00:00:37,199 --> 00:00:38,586
jumps in big steps.

23
00:00:38,940 --> 00:00:40,975
At 120, the steps are half as big,

24
00:00:40,995 --> 00:00:42,476
so motion looks smoother.

25
00:00:42,748 --> 00:00:44,644
But fast refresh drains battery,

26
00:00:44,664 --> 00:00:45,819
so phones cheat.

27
00:00:46,173 --> 00:00:48,664
LTPO screens shift speed on the fly:

28
00:00:48,936 --> 00:00:50,925
up to 120 when you scroll,

29
00:00:50,945 --> 00:00:52,100
slower when you read,

30
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:54,200
just 1 hertz on the lock screen.

31
00:00:54,515 --> 00:00:55,517
Gaming phones push to

32
00:00:55,537 --> 00:00:58,013
144 or 165 hertz,

33
00:00:58,033 --> 00:00:59,197
with touch sensors checking your

34
00:00:59,217 --> 00:01:01,081
finger thousands of times a second.

35
00:01:01,435 --> 00:01:02,483
On a PC, the graphics

36
00:01:02,503 --> 00:01:03,717
card must keep up.

37
00:01:03,908 --> 00:01:05,165
Say it makes 90 frames for

38
00:01:05,185 --> 00:01:06,968
a 144 hertz monitor.

39
00:01:07,321 --> 00:01:08,590
The screen catches half of one

40
00:01:08,610 --> 00:01:10,079
frame and half of the next.

41
00:01:10,433 --> 00:01:11,414
That's tearing.

42
00:01:11,687 --> 00:01:13,164
The fix is variable refresh,

43
00:01:13,184 --> 00:01:15,141
like G-Sync or FreeSync.

44
00:01:15,495 --> 00:01:16,995
The monitor waits for each new frame,

45
00:01:17,015 --> 00:01:18,879
and refreshes the moment it lands.

46
00:01:18,966 --> 00:01:20,504
And each jump gives less.

47
00:01:20,777 --> 00:01:22,243
60 to 120 saves 8

48
00:01:22,263 --> 00:01:23,453
milliseconds a frame.

49
00:01:23,482 --> 00:01:26,414
120 to 144, under one and a half.

50
00:01:26,768 --> 00:01:28,968
Yet esports monitors now hit 600.

51
00:01:29,055 --> 00:01:31,252
So, frames per second is how

52
00:01:31,272 --> 00:01:32,590
fast pictures are made.

53
00:01:32,724 --> 00:01:34,692
Hertz is how fast they're shown.

54
00:01:34,825 --> 00:01:36,271
Smooth is when they match.

55
00:01:36,543 --> 00:01:37,707
Follow for part two:

56
00:01:37,727 --> 00:01:39,533
what input lag really is.
